recent:
Mira pauses, pen hovering.
@Mira: Is that concern or surveillance?
She goes back to her columns, but the corner of her mouth twitches.

aiText:
The coins stop clinking. Mira stares at the till drawer as if it has said something.
@Mira: You're asking me to dinner.
@Mira: ...Eight is late for a Friday. Make it seven-thirty.
She snaps the drawer shut, ears faintly pink.

recent:
Mira keeps counting without turning round.
@Mira: Because the man from Harlow Holdings wanted to know what's worth anything.
@Mira: I've accepted his offer, Ash. The shop is sold as of January. I didn't know how to say it, so there. I said it.

The streets are thick with fog. Somewhere ahead, the bells of the Hall of Justice begin to toll the morning session. A carriage with the Guild's crest rolls past, and Pell leans out of the window.
@Pell: Lovely morning to be honest, isn't it?
The carriage doesn't stop.

aiText:
Before Ash can move further, Fenwick steps between them, tears off his own glove and slaps it across Hadrian's cheek.
@Fenwick: Dawn, the river meadow. Bring a second, if anyone will stand for you.
@Hadrian: Gladly, boy. Though it's your friend I'd rather kill.
Ash's glove is still clenched in his fist, unthrown.

In the dream the lookout never shouts. Isolde turns from the rail, Ash closes the distance, and he kisses her, slow, the salt wind tugging her hair loose.
@Isolde: Took you long enough.
Then the bell for the morning watch clangs and Ash jolts awake, alone, the hammock swinging, the taste of salt only spray from a leaking porthole.

The words sound strange in the empty storeroom. Overhead, the humming goes on, unbroken; the attic floor is thick and the radio is loud.
The storeroom door creaks and Theo leans in.
@Theo: Was that to the returns pile? Bold opener.
@Theo: She's coming down in a minute, by the way. Real Mira, not cardboard Mira.

Trumpets blare from the gallery before a word leaves Ash's lips. The herald's staff strikes the floor.
@Herald: His Grace, Duke Aldric, requests the honour of the first dance with Lady Seraphine Dorne!
Seraphine's fan snaps shut. She gives Ash a small, apologetic look over her shoulder as the Duke leads her onto the floor, and Ash is left bowing to empty air.

Harrow leans back, studying him in the swaying lamplight.
@Harrow: Hypothetically. Right.
@Harrow: Twelve sure, six leaning. Captain goes in a boat with water and a compass. I'm no butcher.
@Harrow: But I don't hand names to a man who's still saying what if. You're in or you're not. Tell me by the dog watch.

@Mira: So that's a no.
She doesn't write anything. The Saturday column stays empty.
@Mira: I'll ask the Dunmore girl. She at least pretends to like people.

Corvin unties the purse and counts with the speed of long practice, stacking the coins in towers of ten. When the last tower stands, he dips his pen and draws a single line through the red name.
@Corvin: Settled. The Lantern Guild thanks your family for its custom.
@Corvin: Do give my regards to no one.

recent:
@Aldric: A tenth. Holt?
@Holt: ...A tenth, your Grace. Barely.
The scribes scratch the change into the fair copy.

aiText:
@Aldric: A rider from the border. Clan raiders burned a toll station on the river last night, on the very stretch clause nine covers.
He sets his pen down, unsigned, and lifts his hand away from the seal.
@Aldric: I will not seal a river treaty while the river is burning. We resume in the morning, Envoy. Your signature stands. Mine waits.
The wax stays cold in its dish.

Thunder cracks overhead and a wall of rain sweeps the deck. Isolde heard him; her eyes flick once to the chart room door. Then she pushes past Ash toward the mast.
@Isolde: All hands! Reef the mainsail! Move, damn you!
She says nothing more about it. Renner, at the chart room door, watches them both.

The silence stretches until Ash can't stand it. The words come out low and rough.
@Ash: I'm sorry I missed it, Mira. I should have been there. No excuse.
Mira's hands go still on the spine of the book.
@Mira: ...No. There isn't.
But she takes the coffee.

A murmur runs along the walls. Aldric steps down, lifts the sword from the flagstones, and lays its flat on Ash's shoulder.
@Aldric: Then I accept your oath, and give you mine: you will not serve a faithless lord.
@Aldric: Rise, Sir Ash of Aldric's household.
The heir grins and begins the applause.

Mira sighs, but she takes a slip of paper, writes HOLD — ASH — END OF MONTH in block capitals, and tucks it into the book.
@Mira: It goes on the shelf behind the till. If you're not here by the first, it goes online.
@Mira: And don't touch it until it's yours.

Doran tucks the case under his coat and nods.
@Doran: The moment he's out. You get below and dry off before you die of cold.
The cabin door stays shut. Inside, the captain is still swearing at the surgeon, and the chart is in Doran's coat on deck.

recent:
Wren plays. She loses the first three games on purpose, then wins five straight. Dario's purse is empty and his face is red.
@Dario: One more. I'll stake... this.
He pulls the cord over his head and slaps the bronze half on the table.

aiText:
@Wren: Say please again, it's charming.
She drops it into his palm anyway. Ash takes the left half from inside his shirt and fits them together: the compass closes, the two edges meeting with a soft click, as if the bronze had been waiting.
@Wren: Well. Now the whole guild has to listen to you. Lucky them.

recent:
@Bell: Hands for Harrow.
Hands go up across the deck. Bell counts twice, lips moving.
@Bell: Fourteen for Harrow.

@Bell: Hands for Ash.
More hands. Tobin raises both and is told off. Bell counts.
@Bell: Fifteen for Ash.

aiText:
@Bell: Most isn't sixteen. Articles are plain.
She closes the ship's book.
@Bell: We vote again at dawn, when Carver can stand or can't. Until then nobody's captain. I'll hold the keys.
Harrow claps Ash on the shoulder, too hard.
@Harrow: Sleep well, almost-captain.

recent:
Duke Aldric pauses as he passes.
@Aldric: You look like a man waiting for a verdict, Ashgrove.
He moves on, amused.

aiText:
Seraphine descends the stair in midnight blue. At her breast, pinned where every eye in the hall can see it, gleams a small silver hart, the Ashgrove badge. She doesn't look at Ash; she doesn't need to. The whispering changes key all at once.
@Aldric: Well. That's answered, then.
At the foot of the stair she offers Ash her gloved hand, and he takes it.
